right?

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:15.920
Yes, exactly. So as long as you have

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:17.840
like a view in your database that is a

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:19.240
accessible,

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.480
uh that is read readable, and you have

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:22.840
like some materialized view on it that

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:24.320
is secure, doesn't have anything like

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:26.000
PII data or something along that, or at

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:27.800
least require like authenticated users

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.320
to access it,

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.840
then you can go ahead and go access this

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.280
along the way.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.160
Um it does require some consideration of

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.480
exactly how you do it. The easiest way

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.120
to do it is a materialized view if you

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:40.120
want to do it securely on your actual

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:41.640
data, so you can go configure that in a

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:43.600
good way.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.240
Um but once you can go do that, now

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:47.720
you're able to build a pipeline that is

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.320
mostly just workflows in your back end

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:52.760
that is issuing work at one after

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:53.760
another.

